---
import EnglishBreadcrumb from '../../../../components/english/EnglishBreadcrumb.astro'
import { OltpVsOlapAccessLab } from '../../../../components/english/OltpVsOlapAccessLab'
import EnglishArticleLayout from '../../../../layouts/EnglishArticleLayout.astro'
import oltpOlapStylesUrl from '../../../../styles/layouts/english-oltp-olap.css?url'
import { getOltpOlapAccessModel } from '../../../../features/english-oltp-olap/model'
import { getEnglishRoute } from '../../../../utils/english-routes'

const title = 'OLTP vs OLAP: Row and Column Storage Explained | sql.sb'
const description =
  'Compare modeled block reads for a point update and a narrow analytical scan. Learn what row- and column-oriented layouts change—and what this teaching model cannot promise.'
const accessModel = getOltpOlapAccessModel()
const currency = new Intl.NumberFormat('en-US', {
  style: 'currency',
  currency: 'CNY',
  minimumFractionDigits: 2,
  maximumFractionDigits: 2,
})
---

<EnglishArticleLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  active="warehouse"
  styleUrls={[oltpOlapStylesUrl]}
>
  <EnglishBreadcrumb
    items={[
      { label: 'English home', key: 'overview' },
      { label: 'Data warehouse', key: 'warehouse' },
      { label: 'OLTP vs OLAP' },
    ]}
  />

  <header class="english-article__header">
    <p class="english-article__eyebrow">Data warehouse · workload and storage</p>
    <h1 class="english-article__title">OLTP vs. OLAP: how row and column layouts change access</h1>
    <p class="english-article__lede">
      The useful distinction is not that one database can transact and another cannot. It is how a
      request tends to access records: a small number of complete rows, or a narrow projection
      across many rows.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="english-answer">
    <span class="english-answer__label">Quick answer</span>
    <p>
      Online transaction processing (OLTP) and online analytical processing (OLAP) describe common
      workload patterns, not two mutually exclusive database types. Row-oriented layouts often keep
      a complete record together; column-oriented layouts often let a scan read only selected
      fields. In the fixed fixture below, retrieving one full row reads one row page or four column
      blocks, while summing one field across eight rows reads four row pages or one column block.
      These are modeled block counts, not bytes, latency, benchmark results, or guarantees about a
      product.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="english-article__body">
    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="workload-shapes">
      <h2 id="workload-shapes">Start with the shape of the request</h2>
      <p>
        An operational request might look up one account's current snapshot and inspect or correct a
        few fields. An analytical request might total a balance field for many accounts while
        ignoring unrelated fields. OLTP is short for online transaction processing; OLAP is online
        analytical processing. These labels describe typical priorities and access patterns—not a
        rule that a warehouse cannot handle a transaction, or that every operational database uses
        rows and every analytical system uses columns.
      </p>
      <p>
        Row and column orientation describe how values are organized for access. They do not, by
        themselves, define isolation, durability, update guarantees, or an application's fit for a
        workload. Hybrid layouts and engine-specific execution strategies also exist.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="fixed-fixture">
      <h2 id="fixed-fixture">A fixed snapshot makes the arithmetic checkable</h2>
      <p>
        This teaching fixture contains one account-balance snapshot for{' '}
        <code>{accessModel.snapshotDate}</code>. Each row is one account's balance at that date; the
        date is held constant so the example's total compares like with like. For more on stating
        what a row means, see <a href={getEnglishRoute('grain')}>data warehouse grain</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="english-table-scroll">
        <table class="english-table" data-access-fixture>
          <caption>Eight account rows · one snapshot date · balances in CNY</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              {accessModel.columns.map((column) => (
                <th scope="col">{column.label}</th>
              ))}
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {accessModel.rows.map((row) => (
              <tr data-account-id={row.accountId}>
                <th scope="row">{row.accountId}</th>
                <td>{row.branch}</td>
                <td>{row.product}</td>
                <td>{currency.format(row.balanceCny)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
          <tfoot>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row" colspan="3">
                Total balance · {accessModel.snapshotDate}
              </th>
              <td>
                {currency.format(
                  accessModel.rows.reduce((total, row) => total + row.balanceCny, 0),
                )}
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tfoot>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        All four fields count as one equal-width value slot in this deliberately small model. A
        block holds eight slots: a row page packs two complete four-field records; a column block
        packs eight values from one field. Thus both layouts store the same 32 fixture values in
        four blocks before any request is run.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="compare-access">
      <h2 id="compare-access">Compare one point access with a narrow scan</h2>
      <p>
        Choose a request to see which blocks the same fixture touches in each layout. For the point
        example, assume a locator has already identified account <code>A-105</code>; index and
        locator reads are not counted. The request inspects all four fields before a possible
        balance correction. The analytical example reads only <code>balance_cny</code> for all eight
        rows.
      </p>
      <OltpVsOlapAccessLab scenarios={accessModel.scenarios} client:load />
      <p>
        Here, modeled read amplification is{' '}
        <code>value slots touched ÷ useful projected values</code>. For a point request, that is 8 ÷
        4 = 2× for the row layout and 32 ÷ 4 = 8× for the column layout. For the scan, it is 32 ÷ 8
        = 4× versus 8 ÷ 8 = 1×. The estimate counts equal-sized teaching slots; it is not a
        measurement of physical bytes or device I/O.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="why-layout-matters">
      <h2 id="why-layout-matters">Why the layouts favor different access shapes</h2>
      <div class="english-table-scroll">
        <table class="english-table">
          <caption>What the fixed layout makes local to a request</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Request shape</th>
              <th scope="col">Row-oriented layout</th>
              <th scope="col">Column-oriented layout</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Read one complete record</th>
              <td>Fields for nearby records share a row page.</td>
              <td>Requested fields live in separate column blocks.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Scan one field across many records</th>
              <td>Pages also carry the other fields in each row.</td>
              <td>The scan can focus on blocks for the projected field.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Correct one field</th>
              <td>The row is colocated in this model; write mechanics are excluded.</td>
              <td>A column-oriented engine may use different mutation strategies.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        This is an access-locality explanation, not a choice rule that applies to every engine. A
        narrow query can still read extra data because of its filter, organization, or execution
        plan; a row layout can still support analytical queries. For a separate question about
        shared warehouse responsibilities, see{' '}
        <a href={getEnglishRoute('warehouseLayers')}>data warehouse layers</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="limits">
      <h2 id="limits">What this model leaves out</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Indexes and locators:</strong> the point example begins after the target row has
          been identified; finding it can itself require reads.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Physical representation:</strong> real page formats, field widths, compression,
          encodings, row groups, and metadata can change bytes and block counts.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Execution context:</strong> caching, partitioning, clustering, concurrency, and
          engine-specific pruning can alter the work a query performs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Writes and transaction semantics:</strong> this comparison counts reads only; it
          does not model mutation cost, logging, isolation, durability, or concurrent updates.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="english-callout" data-tone="info">
        <strong>Do not turn a workload label into a universal capability claim</strong>
        <p>
          Whether a particular warehouse supports a transaction pattern depends on that product's
          write, concurrency, and consistency contracts. Row-versus-column organization alone cannot
          answer whether it is suitable for an application workload.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        If the question is instead whether repeating a pipeline write changes its result, see{' '}
        <a href={getEnglishRoute('idempotentEtl')}>idempotent ETL</a>. Write repeatability and
        physical read layout are related engineering concerns, but they are not the same decision.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="english-article__section" aria-labelledby="decision-checklist">
      <h2 id="decision-checklist">A practical comparison checklist</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Write down the actual predicates, projected fields, and update scope.</li>
        <li>Identify which pages or column groups the query plan says it reads.</li>
        <li>Separate logical values requested from physical bytes and elapsed time.</li>
        <li>
          Test representative data, caching state, and concurrent workload on the target engine.
        </li>
        <li>Compare the engine's documented write and consistency guarantees separately.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="english-related" aria-labelledby="related">
      <h2 id="related">Related pages</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('warehouse')}>Data warehouse engineering</a>
          <p>Browse the English pages on warehouse modeling, pipelines, and reliability.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('grain')}>Data warehouse grain</a>
          <p>State what one row represents before comparing how its values are stored.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('warehouseLayers')}>Data warehouse layers</a>
          <p>Compare responsibilities and dependencies, not row-versus-column access.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={getEnglishRoute('idempotentEtl')}>Idempotent ETL</a>
          <p>Understand repeatable writes, retries, and reruns as a separate concern.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
</EnglishArticleLayout>
